## 1. Semisimple finite source

Let

```text
A = direct_sum_(alpha=1)^r M_(n_alpha)(C)
```

be a finite-dimensional semisimple star algebra with positive weighted trace.
For a self-adjoint curvature element `F=(F_alpha)`, define its canonical central
and traceless parts blockwise:

```text
lambda_alpha = Tr(F_alpha)/n_alpha,
z = direct_sum lambda_alpha I_(n_alpha),
F_0 = F-z.
```

Then `z` lies in the center `Z(A)` and every block of `F_0` has trace zero.

## 2. Orthogonal central decomposition

### Theorem 2.1

The decomposition

```text
F=z+F_0
```

is unique and orthogonal for the trace pairing. It is the conditional
expectation of `F` onto the center followed by its orthogonal complement.

### Proof

The matrix trace makes scalar and traceless matrices orthogonal in every simple
block. Direct summation preserves orthogonality. Uniqueness follows because the
intersection of the scalar and traceless block subspaces is zero. QED.

## 3. Gauge-blind center theorem

### Theorem 3.1

For every `X in A`,

```text
[F,X]=[F_0,X].
```

Moreover, the kernel of

```text
F |-> ad_F
```

is exactly `Z(A)`. Therefore adjoint, gauge, automorphism-generator, and
commutator data determine `F` only modulo `r` real central coordinates before
additional symmetry is imposed.

### Proof

Central elements commute with all `X`, proving the first identity. Conversely,
an element whose commutator vanishes on all of `A` is central by definition.
The self-adjoint center of the displayed semisimple algebra has one real scalar
per simple summand. QED.

This is an identifiability no-go. Repeating a search over Lie brackets or gauge
orbits cannot recover the missing central magnitudes.

## 4. Where the missing scalars remain visible

The Jordan action does retain the center:

```text
C_F(X)=F X+X F
      =C_(F_0)(X)+C_z(X).
```

On block `alpha`,

```text
C_z(X_alpha)=2 lambda_alpha X_alpha.
```

In particular,

```text
F=1/2 C_F(1).
```

Thus the complete symmetric action, residual Hessian, or a normalized value
functional can recover data that the gauge/commutator shadow necessarily
forgets. This locates the unresolved scalar problem in the action/Jordan/value
layer, not in another automorphism calculation.

## 5. Symmetry compression

Let a finite group act by complex-linear star automorphisms and permute isomorphic simple
blocks. The invariant self-adjoint center has one real coordinate for each
orbit of blocks. Therefore

```text
number of central source scalars
  = number of block orbits,
```

before further normalization or source equations.

Examples:

- a simple full matrix algebra has one central scalar;
- three inequivalent sector blocks have at most three central scalars;
- a transitive symmetry relating three isomorphic blocks reduces them to one.

This gives a rigorous route by which a large matrix can retain only one to
three universal scalar primitives. It does not prove that the current MTT
sector algebra or symmetry has that form.

## 6. Consequence for the 27 carrier

The full endomorphism algebra of one irreducible 27-dimensional complex carrier
is `M_27(C)` and has a one-dimensional center. If the accepted finite carrier
uses this full simple algebra irreducibly, all commutator data are blind to one
common scalar normalization. If instead the physical operator algebra is a
proper direct sum or has superselection blocks, the center can be larger.

The correct parameter count therefore comes from the commutant and central
decomposition of the selected operator algebra, not from the matrix dimension
`27 x 27` itself.

This statement is conditional: the existing family-qutrit 27, exceptional
Jordan 27, and physical finite carrier must not be identified without their
accepted intertwiner and central-character tests.

## 7. Source-language refinement

A preprojection source should record both complementary readouts:

```text
Lie shadow:     ad_F       -> orientation, gauge response, automorphisms;
Jordan shadow:  C_F        -> stability pressure and central magnitudes.
```

The trace normalization and admissible block decomposition are part of source
typing. Otherwise the central coordinates can be rescaled or repartitioned and
are not predictions.

## 8. Falsifiers and limits

This theorem does not show that an MTT scalar is a central curvature value. The
candidate fails if:

- the selected physical operator algebra is not the claimed semisimple algebra;
- the scalar rows do not enter through the Jordan/action side;
- the trace or real structure is not selected;
- the symmetry does not identify the proposed blocks; or
- empirical values are inserted to choose `lambda_alpha`.

Even when only one central primitive remains, its numerical value still needs
a selected source equation or normalization theorem.

